import{_ as a,o as s,c as i,ao as t}from"./chunks/framework.BGQaYrLm.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Common pitfalls","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"pitfalls.md","filePath":"pitfalls.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"pitfalls.md"};function n(l,e,r,d,h,p){return s(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t(`<h1 id="Common-pitfalls" tabindex="-1">Common pitfalls <a class="header-anchor" href="#Common-pitfalls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Common pitfalls {#Common-pitfalls}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A short guide to the things that most often trip people up when fitting GLLVMs.</p><h2 id="Latent-factors-are-identified-only-up-to-rotation-and-sign" tabindex="-1">Latent factors are identified only up to rotation and sign <a class="header-anchor" href="#Latent-factors-are-identified-only-up-to-rotation-and-sign" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Latent factors are identified only up to rotation and sign {#Latent-factors-are-identified-only-up-to-rotation-and-sign}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The loadings <code>Λ</code> and the factor scores are not unique: any rotation <code>Λ → Λ R</code> (with <code>R</code> orthogonal) gives the same fit. So <strong>do not compare raw <code>Λ</code> across fits or against a known truth</strong> — compare rotation-invariant quantities instead:</p><ul><li><p>the model-implied covariance <code>Σ_y = Λ Λᵀ + Ψ</code> (<code>sigma_y_site</code>),</p></li><li><p>per-response communalities (<code>communality</code>),</p></li><li><p>cross-response correlations (<code>correlation</code>).</p></li></ul><p>For a fixed, interpretable orientation, rotate the loadings (e.g. varimax) <em>after</em> fitting.</p><h2 id="Choose-the-number-of-factors-K-deliberately" tabindex="-1">Choose the number of factors <code>K</code> deliberately <a class="header-anchor" href="#Choose-the-number-of-factors-K-deliberately" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choose the number of factors \`K\` deliberately {#Choose-the-number-of-factors-K-deliberately}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Too few factors miss real structure; too many invite over-fitting and weak identifiability. Compare a few values of <code>K</code> by log-likelihood / information criteria, and prefer the smallest <code>K</code> that captures the covariance you care about.</p><h2 id="Check-convergence" tabindex="-1">Check convergence <a class="header-anchor" href="#Check-convergence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check convergence {#Check-convergence}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>fit_gaussian_gllvm</code> warm-starts from PPCA and usually converges in a step or two; the non-Gaussian fitters use L-BFGS over a Laplace marginal. Always check the <code>converged</code> flag — if it is <code>false</code>, try a different start or more iterations. Standardising responses to a common scale helps the Gaussian path.</p><h2 id="Use-the-Op-path-for-large-phylogenies" tabindex="-1">Use the O(p) path for large phylogenies <a class="header-anchor" href="#Use-the-Op-path-for-large-phylogenies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Use the O(p) path for large phylogenies {#Use-the-Op-path-for-large-phylogenies}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The dense Gaussian fit with a phylogenetic covariance is <code>O(p³)</code> and assumes <code>n ≥ p</code>. For many species, use the O(p) phylogenetic fitter <a href="/GLLVModels.jl/previews/PR436/api#GLLVModels.fit_phylo_gaussian"><code>fit_phylo_gaussian</code></a>, which scales linearly (≈ 0.8 ms per gradient at p = 10,000) by never forming the dense <code>p×p</code> covariance.</p><h2 id="Binary-data:-watch-for-separation" tabindex="-1">Binary data: watch for separation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Binary-data:-watch-for-separation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Binary data: watch for separation {#Binary-data:-watch-for-separation}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With binary responses, a response that is all-0 or all-1 drives its intercept to ±∞ (complete separation). <code>fit_binomial_gllvm</code> clamps the linear predictor for numerical safety, but a non-convergence flag or an extreme intercept is a sign to inspect that response.</p><p>See also: <a href="./quickstart">Get started</a> · <a href="./response-families">Response families</a> · <a href="./api">Reference</a>.</p><h2 id="Loading-StatsBase-or-MixedModels-alongside-GLLVModels-breaks-six-verbs" tabindex="-1">Loading StatsBase or MixedModels alongside GLLVModels breaks six verbs <a class="header-anchor" href="#Loading-StatsBase-or-MixedModels-alongside-GLLVModels-breaks-six-verbs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Loading StatsBase or MixedModels alongside GLLVModels breaks six verbs {#Loading-StatsBase-or-MixedModels-alongside-GLLVModels-breaks-six-verbs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>GLLVModels.jl defines its own <code>confint</code>, <code>aic</code>, <code>bic</code>, <code>predict</code>, <code>fitted</code> and <code>residuals</code>. It does not extend the StatsAPI generics of the same name, so if you load another modelling package in the same session, Julia sees two unrelated functions sharing one name and refuses to pick:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">using</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> GLLVModels, StatsBase</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">confint</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(fit, Y; method </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> :wald</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># WARNING: both StatsBase and GLLVModels export &quot;confint&quot;;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">#          uses of it in module Main must be qualified</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># ERROR: UndefVarError: \`confint\` not defined</span></span></code></pre></div><p>All six behave this way, and <strong><code>StatsBase</code> is not the only trigger</strong> — <code>MixedModels.jl</code> exports the same six, so the comparison workflow the README suggests runs into it too. <code>DataFrames</code> and <code>Distributions</code> do not.</p><p><strong>The fix is to qualify the call:</strong></p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">using</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> GLLVModels, StatsBase</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">GLLVModels</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">confint</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(fit, Y; method </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> :wald</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)     </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># GLLVModels&#39;s interval machinery</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">GLLVModels</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">predict</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(fit, Y; type </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> :link</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">GLLVModels</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">aic</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(fit)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Or import only what you need from the other package (<code>using StatsBase: mean, sample</code>) so the names never collide.</p><p>Nothing is broken about the fit itself — this is a name-resolution problem, and both functions remain reachable when qualified. The examples elsewhere in these docs are written unqualified because they assume <code>using GLLVModels</code> alone.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">This is a known wart, not a design choice</p><p>Re-rooting these six onto the StatsAPI generics would remove the clash entirely and let generic ecosystem code work with a <code>GllvmFit</code>. That is an API change, so the present behaviour remains explicit and predictable.</p></div><p>The one genuinely deliberate name clash is <code>Multinomial</code>, which is documented separately under <a href="./response-families">Response families</a> — always write <code>GLLVModels.Multinomial()</code> for the family marker and <code>Distributions.Multinomial(...)</code> for the count-vector law.</p>`,25)])])}const g=a(o,[["render",n]]);export{k as __pageData,g as default};
